{"statement": "Accelerationist argues that fast iterative trials match tech speed while pre-deployment rules build walls that crush open-source developers and lock in corporate monopolies. That argument gets one crucial fact right: rigid pre-deployment mandates do indeed hand compliance moats to dominant firms like OpenAI and Google. But pretending that market flexibility stops industry capture ignores how venture capital firms already write the software that writes the software. The reality is that neither fast trials nor rigid mandates protect the people doing the physical work to keep these systems running. There is a twenty-three-year-old worker in Nairobi labeling violent content for two dollars an hour so these models pass safety audits, and neither regulatory regime touches their labor conditions.\n\nHorizontal agencies like the Federal Trade Commission lack the technical domain expertise required to enforce specialized safety rules, forcing them to rely entirely on corporate self-reporting. When the FTC attempted to regulate specialized technical domains without an internal engineering workforce during the nineteen-nineties antitrust pushes, the result was a toothless compliance theater that shielded incumbents from liability. A comprehensive pre-deployment mandate handed to an understaffed agency simply delegates enforcement power back to the corporations being watched. The enforcer becomes a rubber stamp for the regulated entity because the state does not employ the engineers needed to verify the code.\n\nEntrenched corporate lobbies use administrative complexity to calcify regulatory frameworks and lock out independent developers. Historical parallels from the telecommunications act of nineteen-nineties show that complex compliance costs function as market entry barriers while preserving monopoly rents. When lawmakers demand heavy pre-deployment audits, they build a toll booth that only trillion-dollar balance sheets can afford to clear. The policy choice is not between public safety and market speed; it is between transparent democratic control and corporate self-governance dressed up as compliance. Who pays the regulatory compliance tax when the dust settles, and who collects the rent?\n\nIf horizontal agencies lack domain expertise, does dropping pre-deployment mandates leave the public entirely exposed to unvetted frontier models during rapid iteration cycles? The answer is that the public is already exposed to unvetted models while regulators debate paperwork. The real enforcement mechanism is not a government stamp before launch, but liability laws that hold corporations financially responsible for downstream harms. When a model fails in deployment, the developer must face uncapped civil liability rather than hiding behind a pre-deployment certification. Why should taxpayers fund an agency to bless software that its creators refuse to guarantee?",
